In this lesson, we'll look at solving linear equations, which are equations where the variable only appears to the first power. The big idea is simple: you just undo whatever has been done to the variable, one step at a time. First, if there are any brackets, expand them. Then collect all the variable terms on one side and the plain numbers on the other side, and combine any like terms together. Once the variable is alone on one side, divide by whatever number is sitting in front of it, and you've got your answer. Each of those steps works because adding or subtracting the same thing from both sides keeps the equation balanced, and so does multiplying or dividing by the same non-zero number. If the variable shows up on both sides, don't panic. Just subtract it from one side to bring everything together before you continue. And finally, always pop your answer back into the original equation to check it works. Now let's see it in action.